      "transcript": {
        "language": "en",
        "quality": "verified",
        "sentences": [
          { "id": "s001", "speaker": "N", "text": "Good morning, everyone. Today I want to talk about something we all do but rarely think about: sleep.", "startMs": 0, "endMs": 9528, "align": "manual" },
          { "id": "s002", "speaker": "N", "text": "For a long time, sleep was regarded as a passive state, a kind of shutdown after a busy day.", "startMs": 9528, "endMs": 17792, "align": "manual" },
          { "id": "s003", "speaker": "N", "text": "Recent research, however, suggests that the brain remains remarkably active during sleep.", "startMs": 17792, "endMs": 25820, "align": "manual" },
          { "id": "s004", "speaker": "N", "text": "In one experiment, students learned a list of word pairs in the afternoon.", "startMs": 25820, "endMs": 31954, "align": "manual" },
          { "id": "s005", "speaker": "N", "text": "Half of them slept for ninety minutes, while the other half stayed awake.", "startMs": 31954, "endMs": 37972, "align": "manual" },
          { "id": "s006", "speaker": "N", "text": "The group that slept recalled about twenty percent more pairs the next morning.", "startMs": 37972, "endMs": 43761, "align": "manual" },
          { "id": "s007", "speaker": "N", "text": "What seems to matter is not simply the total amount of sleep, but the deep stages of it.", "startMs": 43761, "endMs": 50729, "align": "manual" },
          { "id": "s008", "speaker": "N", "text": "So if you are preparing for an exam, a short nap may be more useful than another hour of cramming.", "startMs": 50729, "endMs": 58387, "align": "manual" }
        ]
      }
    },

    {
      "materialId": "mat-demo-R1",
      "section": "reading",
      "part": "reading.p1",
      "sharedAcrossVariants": true,
      "passage": {
        "title": "Remote work",
        "wordCount": 66,
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "id": "P1",
            "text": "Remote work was once a rare privilege. Today, millions of employees {{1}} at least part of their week from home. Companies report that the arrangement reduces commuting costs and gives workers more control over their schedules. Critics, {{2}}, warn that isolation can weaken teamwork and slow down informal communication. Whether the benefits outweigh these costs remains a {{3}} question."
          }
        ]
      },
      "wordBank": ["work", "however", "disputed", "travel", "therefore", "settled"]
    },

    {
      "materialId": "mat-demo-R2",
      "section": "reading",
      "part": "reading.p2",
      "sharedAcrossVariants": true,
      "passage": {
        "title": "The return of the paper notebook",
        "wordCount": 148,
        "paragraphs": [
          { "id": "A", "text": "For a decade, schools have been moving lessons onto screens. Tablets replaced textbooks, and homework moved into the cloud. The change was celebrated as efficiency." },
          { "id": "B", "text": "Yet a growing number of teachers are asking students to bring a plain notebook to class. They argue that handwriting forces learners to slow down and decide what matters." },
          { "id": "C", "text": "Researchers who study note-taking point to a similar effect. Students who type tend to record lectures word for word, while those who write by hand summarise and connect ideas." },
          { "id": "D", "text": "None of this means screens are harmful in themselves. The real issue, teachers say, is whether a tool encourages attention or simply makes copying easier." }
        ]
      }
    },

    {
      "materialId": "mat-demo-R3",
      "section": "reading",
      "part": "reading.p3",
      "sharedAcrossVariants": true,
      "passage": {
        "title": "The quiet cost of convenience",
        "wordCount": 155,
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "id": "P1",
            "text": "Food delivery apps have changed the way cities eat. A meal that once required leaving the office can now arrive in twenty minutes. The convenience is real, but it is not free. Couriers are paid per delivery, which means their income depends on speed rather than care. Restaurants, meanwhile, surrender up to thirty percent of each order to the platform. And the packaging that keeps food warm ends up, almost always, in landfill. None of these costs appears on the receipt. Economists call this an externality: a cost borne by someone other than the buyer and the seller. The difficulty is that externalities are invisible by design. A customer comparing two restaurants sees only price and rating, not the wages behind them. Some cities have begun to require platforms to disclose courier pay and packaging waste. Whether disclosure changes behaviour is still unclear, but it at least makes the hidden visible."
          }
        ]
      }
    },

    {
      "materialId": "mat-demo-T1",
      "section": "translation",
      "part": "translation",
      "sharedAcrossVariants": true,
      "passage": {
        "title": "汉译英",
        "wordCount": 78,
        "paragraphs": [
          { "id": "P1", "text": "中国的移动支付普及率位居世界前列。无论是在城市还是乡村，人们都可以用手机完成购物、交通和缴费。这种便利不仅改变了消费习惯，也推动了数字经济的发展。" }
        ]
      },
      "referenceTranslation": {
        "text": "China ranks among the world's leaders in the adoption of mobile payment. Whether in cities or in the countryside, people can use their phones to shop, pay for transport and settle utility bills. This convenience has not only changed spending habits but also driven the growth of the digital economy.",
        "sourceId": "src-demo-synthetic"
      }
    }
