      "referenceEssays": [
        {
          "sourceId": "src-demo-synthetic",
          "band": "sample",
          "text": "Universities have long debated whether students should be pushed beyond their chosen field. In my view, a limited requirement outside the major is worthwhile, provided that it does not crowd out core study.\n\nThe strongest argument is that most real problems cross disciplinary lines. A software engineer who has taken a course in ethics is better prepared to ask who bears the cost of an automated decision. A biology student who has studied statistics is less likely to misread a clinical trial.\n\nOpponents worry that extra courses dilute expertise, and this concern is fair. The solution, however, is careful design rather than abandonment: two or three well-chosen modules, taken early, cost little and widen the frame through which students see their own subject.\n\nA degree should train a specialist, but it should also produce someone who can talk to people outside the specialism. A modest requirement outside the major serves both goals."
